# Copy to .env and fill in what your providers need. Every value here is
# deployment configuration; no credential belongs in this file.
#
# The credentials themselves are FILES, read by the broker from the directory
# mounted at /secrets — never environment variables. That is deliberate: an
# env var is visible to anything that can read the process, and the broker's
# whole job is to be the only thing holding them.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the basics

# Which optional services run. `observer` is the audit-trail dashboard.
#
# KEEP THIS LINE. Compose reads an absent COMPOSE_PROFILES as "no profiles
# enabled", so deleting it silently drops the observer — the trail is still
# written and rotated, but nothing serves it and nothing says so. To turn the
# dashboard off deliberately, set it empty rather than removing it:
#
#   COMPOSE_PROFILES=
#
# and back on without a restart of anything else:
#
#   docker compose --profile observer up -d observer
COMPOSE_PROFILES=observer

# Host port for the observer dashboard, on 127.0.0.1 only.
#
# Leave it commented for 9000, the historical default. Set it EMPTY —
# `OBSERVER_PORT=` — to have Docker assign a free port instead, which is what
# you want when several deployments share a machine; read back which one with
#
#   docker compose port observer 9000
#
# A number works too, but then something has to remember which deployment owns
# which port, and that is state that can be wrong.
# OBSERVER_PORT=9000

# Where your credential files live on the host. The broker mounts this
# read-only; the lab container never sees it.
# AGENT_CREDS_DIR=${HOME}/.config/agent-creds

# What the agent works on, mounted at /workspace in the lab container.
# WORKSPACE_DIR=./workspace

# Set to false ONLY for tooling that cannot use an HTTP proxy at all — raw
# sockets, ssh, anything that resolves before proxying. It costs you egress
# mediation; the broker stays unreachable either way.
# LAB_INTERNAL=true

# Hostnames the proxy refuses to forward to. Change this if you renamed the
# services. Nothing the lab container sends can widen it.
# POLICY_INTERNAL_HOSTS=broker,cred-gateway

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- providers
#
# Nothing provider-specific is listed here, and that is deliberate.
#
# Every bank entry declares what it needs in its own `bank/<name>/provider.json`:
# `secrets[].env` and `secrets[].file` give the variable and the filename, and
# `config` gives any settings the broker or proxy read. Restating them here
# would make this file a second and staler copy — the thing #95 removed from
# compose.yaml, one layer down. It would also enumerate exactly the entries
# that existed the day it was written.
#
# So: install an entry, read its manifest, add its variables below. The shape,
# using the placeholder name from template/provider/static-key:
#
#   ACME_TOKEN_PATH=/secrets/acme.token     # secrets[].env = /secrets/ + secrets[].file
#
# A tool does the same from the manifest and needs no per-provider code, which
# is the property the bank exists to give.
#
# Anything the LAB container needs goes in lab.env instead, not here.
